// PURPOSE: Mark the Dart-side entry point of the iOS ink package and state exactly how far the
//          implementation has got, so nobody imports this package expecting a working pen.
// INPUTS: None.
// OUTPUTS: A library with no public API yet.
// KEYWORDS: ink-native, ios, pencilkit, spike-s2, no-api-yet, honest-scaffold
//
// Present today: the native scaffolding generated by `flutter create` — `InkNativeIosPlugin`
// in Swift, its podspec and its Package.swift, all registered through the `flutter.plugin`
// section of pubspec.yaml. That is enough for the plugin to be built into an iOS app.
//
// Not present: the Dart class that would implement InkNativePlatform and mediate between Dart
// and Swift. Until it exists this package cannot satisfy the ink contract, and the app-facing
// `ink_native` package therefore falls back to the no-op implementation even on iOS.
//
// The Dart implementation is spike S2 work. It is deliberately not stubbed here: a stub that
// returned empty results would let a caller believe ink was enabled on iOS and silently discard
// every stroke, which is worse than the current state, where the editor can see that ink is
// unsupported.

/// iOS ink implementation, backed by PencilKit. No public API yet; see the file header.
library;
